28
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:14,540
- Let's go.
- I need to meet the dinosaurs.

29
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,340
I'm excited.

30
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:18,500
An hour and 40 minutes.

31
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:25,270
Ju Bin, you've been acting for a while now.

32
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:26,470
- Yes.
- For a long time.

33
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:28,540
You've done a lot of impressionable acting.

34
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:30,040
I've seen quite a lot of it.

35
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:32,140
My second role...

36
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:34,200
was in "Mr. Sunshine." I auditioned for it.

37
00:01:34,570 --> 00:01:36,240
I think I was very lucky.

38
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:38,440
I've also worked a lot with famous directors.

39
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:40,600
But they don't do well when they work with me.

40
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:42,600
- What did you do?
- Seriously.

41
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:44,700
Their next work always becomes a hit.

42
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:47,270
Before "Queen of Tears," there was "Bulgasal."

43
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,540
- "Bulgasal." - Did that do well?

44
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:52,170
Then right after that was "Queen of Tears."

45
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:54,970
Also, Director Cha Young Hoon...

46
00:01:54,970 --> 00:01:56,500
did "When the Camellia Blooms."

47
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:58,570
- Oh, gosh.
- Oh, my.

48
00:01:58,570 --> 00:02:00,070
When they work with me, things don't go well.

49
00:02:00,070 --> 00:02:03,100
Directors must be experimenting with you,

50
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:05,240
- and then doing the real thing.
- Then they go do other works.

51
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:07,900
The ones after one are always a hit.

52
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:09,340
Can you tell me which work?

53
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:11,470
To prepare.

54
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:13,200
- To join the project after mine?
- Yes.

55
00:02:14,470 --> 00:02:16,940
If they worked with Joon, it's a guarantee of success.

56
00:02:17,070 --> 00:02:18,270
That's right.

57
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,100
I've never said this anywhere,

58
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:23,770
the deceased Director An Pan Seok...

59
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:26,440
told me this, and it has stayed in my memory.

60
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:27,570
What did he say?

61
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:29,870
"What work will you do next?"

62
00:02:29,870 --> 00:02:32,470
"Have you thought of anything?" When he asked, I responded,

63
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:35,540
"I'm planning to think on it..."

64
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:38,400
"and get some rest."

65
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:40,340
When I said that, Director An said,

66
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:42,570
"Hey, actors should adjust to dramas."

67
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:43,810
"If dramas adjust to actors,"

68
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:47,400
"you'll only find what you want to work on once every 20 years."

69
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:49,570
- "Are you okay with that?" - He's right about that.

70
00:02:49,570 --> 00:02:51,270
- He's right.
- He's right.

71
00:02:51,270 --> 00:02:53,340
That really hit me.

72
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:54,770
Since then, I've accepted every role I was offered.

73
00:02:55,270 --> 00:02:57,600
It's all about experience.

74
00:02:57,600 --> 00:02:59,070
- Right.
- Whether it's a success or not...

75
00:02:59,070 --> 00:03:00,940
- is not something we can decide.
- Right.

76
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:02,240
In the end, the person who acts...

77
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:04,840
- until they die is the winner.
- Totally.

78
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:06,440
- Totally.
- Correct. Your mindset...

79
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:08,640
is exactly the same as mine.

80
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:11,440
That's right. I'm Jun Hyun Moo of the acting world.

81
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:13,900
I also did everything I was offered.

82
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:16,400
(While they chatted,)

83
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:17,400
(the scenery outside of the window changed.)

84
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:18,770
It's so pretty.

85
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:23,470
It's land, but it feels like the ocean. I can't see the end.

86
00:03:23,470 --> 00:03:24,870
(An endlessly vast land)

87
00:03:25,270 --> 00:03:28,170
It's hard to see the horizon without anything in the way...

88
00:03:28,170 --> 00:03:29,500
in Korea.

89
00:03:30,270 --> 00:03:33,070
Getting able to drive on a road like this...

90
00:03:33,070 --> 00:03:34,270
feels so euphoric.

91
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:37,170
- Oh, my.
- Oh, my.

92
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:39,970
(Ju Bin can't stop looking out the window.)

93
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:41,440
The clouds are melting down.

94
00:03:42,570 --> 00:03:44,740
- That's rain?
- So fascinating.

95
00:03:44,740 --> 00:03:48,800
(From the sky far away, rain falls like dispersing clouds.)

96
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:51,540
We can see that because it's so vast.

97
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:53,400
Seriously. Since it's vast.

98
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:55,400
It's cool how it's only raining there.

99
00:03:55,970 --> 00:03:58,200
Look over there. The ducks. Oh, my.

100
00:03:58,340 --> 00:04:00,270
- Where?
- There are so many ducks there.

101
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:01,800
Oh, you're right.

102
00:04:02,140 --> 00:04:03,940
- Oh, to the right.
- Where?

103
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:05,000
Oh, my!

104
00:04:05,870 --> 00:04:08,500
- The cows.
- It looks fake.

105
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:10,570
Horses.

106
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:11,940
- Oh, horses.
- How cute.

107
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:15,570
(The peaceful scenery welcomes travellers.)

108
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:17,370
I love this.

109
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:20,140
It's all flatlands.

110
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:23,170
(Driving on the monotonous plains with not a single obstacle,)

111
00:04:23,610 --> 00:04:27,040
(Joon slowly starts to get bored.)

112
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:29,610
(He must be tired.)

113
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:31,840
Let me know if you're tired. I'll drive.

114
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:33,970
- If you want to try, go ahead.
- You can try, Ju Bin.

115
00:04:33,970 --> 00:04:35,200
It's just this road the whole time.

116
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:36,500
Let's go.

117
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:39,270
- It accelerates well, right?
- Oh, I love it!

118
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:41,240
- This is fun!
- You drive a bit roughly.

119
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:44,370
- Your driving is a bit rough.
- You drive quite roughly.

120
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:46,300
Not at all.

121
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:51,110
(This 90-minute drive is full of happiness.)

122
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:54,800
(I'm feeling good.)

123
00:04:56,670 --> 00:04:58,000
What is this? Look ahead.

124
00:04:58,000 --> 00:04:59,270
Oh, my. What is that?

125
00:04:59,270 --> 00:05:00,270
Oh, what is that?

126
00:05:00,270 --> 00:05:02,070
- I see layers.
- That's right.

127
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:09,070
(Where is this place with the completely different landscape?)

128
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:12,240
- Oh, that's so cool.
- That's so fascinating.

129
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:14,470
(The scenery here is completely different from Calgary.)

130
00:05:14,470 --> 00:05:15,570
I love it.

131
00:05:15,570 --> 00:05:16,570
(The scenery here is completely different from Calgary.)

132
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:18,340
Oh, my.

133
00:05:21,570 --> 00:05:22,570
(An unexplored world with 70 million years of history)

134
00:05:22,570 --> 00:05:23,770
We can see it so up close.

135
00:05:23,770 --> 00:05:24,940
This feels weird.

136
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:28,170
(An unexplored world with 70 million years of history)

137
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:29,400
It's amazing, isn't it?

138
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:33,700
Drumheller is about an hour and a half from Calgary,

139
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:37,200
and it's one of the coolest towns in the Alberta Badlands.

140
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:41,240
With its insane, almost unreal Badlands landscape...

141
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:43,570
and tons of dinosaur fossils,

142
00:05:43,570 --> 00:05:46,800
it's known as the dinosaur capital of the world.

143
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:47,800
(It's known as the dinosaur capital of the world.)

144
00:05:48,870 --> 00:05:50,600
It really is the dinosaur capital.

145
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:53,100
Where else is there to say? It's the dinosaur capital.

146
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:54,370
(Drumheller, strata formed during the Late Cretaceous period...)

147
00:05:54,370 --> 00:05:55,640
(about 75 million years ago)

148
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:57,640
So dinosaurs used to run around this place.

149
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:01,570
Right. When dinosaurs were around, this would've been underground.

150
00:06:01,740 --> 00:06:05,040
I can imagine dinosaurs in this background.

151
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:06,840
- See this scenery.
- "Jurassic Park."

152
00:06:07,170 --> 00:06:09,070
Oh, this is so cool.

153
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:11,270
Good job, Ju Bin.

154
00:06:11,270 --> 00:06:12,400
It's nothing.

155
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:21,100
What is this place?

156
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:22,670
Isn't it amazing?

157
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:25,240
This feels weird.

158
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:27,670
It feels like we've come to a weird world.

159
00:06:27,900 --> 00:06:29,340
The history...

160
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:30,640
(Speechless)

161
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:33,600
This place is insane.

162
00:06:36,670 --> 00:06:42,100
(Interesting landforms can be spotted all over.)

163
00:06:42,470 --> 00:06:45,500
That mushroom-shaped thing...

164
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:46,900
What is that?

165
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:48,340
Is it a mushroom?

166
00:06:48,340 --> 00:06:49,570
- Everyone.
- Oh, my.

167
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:51,040
This is a hoodoo.

168
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:52,970
It's shaped like a mushroom, right?

169
00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:54,870
So a hoodoo...

170
00:06:54,870 --> 00:06:56,240
is a sandstone.

171
00:06:57,140 --> 00:07:00,170
They're spires made of sand.

172
00:07:00,170 --> 00:07:02,640
The spires are about 5m to 7m tall.

173
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:04,900
The reason they're shaped like mushrooms...

174
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:06,640
The tops look a bit different, right?

175
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:07,870
Yes.

176
00:07:07,870 --> 00:07:09,140
Those aren't sandstones.

177
00:07:09,270 --> 00:07:10,300
What are they?

178
00:07:10,370 --> 00:07:11,740
It's called calcite.

179
00:07:11,740 --> 00:07:13,870
It's a bit harder than sandstone.

180
00:07:14,340 --> 00:07:16,300
They don't erode easily.

181
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:18,640
So they stay, and only the bottom erodes,

182
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:19,740
creating a mushroom shape.

183
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:20,900
(Calcite, sandstone spire)

184
00:07:21,070 --> 00:07:24,440
Every year, 1cm of the hoodoos gets eroded away.

185
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:25,470
- Right.
- Right.

186
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:27,940
Oh, hoodoos is a word from West Africa.

187
00:07:30,070 --> 00:07:32,400
Some indigenous people here...

188
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:34,970
believe that these move at night.

189
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:38,640
They see it as something sacred.

190
00:07:38,640 --> 00:07:40,740
(As if fairies rested here)

191
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:45,640
(Hoodoos create a magical landscape.)

192
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:50,800
I can't believe we can see it up close.

193
00:07:52,670 --> 00:07:55,100
So up close. This feels weird.

194
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:58,770
It's so fascinating that we can go up there.

195
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:00,540
Seriously, they let people go up there.

196
00:08:00,540 --> 00:08:03,200
(Follow us.)

197
00:08:03,570 --> 00:08:07,200
(A special experience of climbing up rocks millions of years old)

198
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:12,670
This place is so fun. It's like a rollercoaster.

199
00:08:13,970 --> 00:08:16,500
Like this. Oh, my.

200
00:08:16,770 --> 00:08:18,540
(Climbing up rhythmically and swiftly)

201
00:08:18,540 --> 00:08:19,740
Oh, nice.

202
00:08:23,470 --> 00:08:25,400
This looks like the place...

203
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:28,870
where Odysseus goes to meet the giants.

204
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:30,700
What was his name?

205
00:08:31,470 --> 00:08:34,270
What is the name of the one-eyed giant...

206
00:08:34,270 --> 00:08:35,970
in "The Odyssey?"

207
00:08:36,770 --> 00:08:38,540
It's Polyphemus Cyclops.

208
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:39,900
- Cyclops.
- Cyclops.

209
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:42,000
The one-eyed giant is my favourite character.

210
00:08:42,070 --> 00:08:43,270
Cyclops.

211
00:08:43,470 --> 00:08:45,100
Oh, my. Look this way.

212
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:46,170
Oh, my.

213
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:49,000
Oh, how cool.

214
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:50,640
This is amazing.

215
00:08:51,470 --> 00:08:52,600
Oh, my.

216
00:08:52,670 --> 00:08:53,840
This is nice.

217
00:08:54,170 --> 00:08:55,970
Oh, my. What is this?

218
00:08:56,210 --> 00:08:57,440
I'm speechless.

219
00:08:57,710 --> 00:08:58,770
It's nice and cool.

220
00:08:59,270 --> 00:09:00,400
So nice.

221
00:09:00,740 --> 00:09:01,800
I love it.

222
00:09:01,970 --> 00:09:03,970
- This is dinosaur capital, right?
- Right.

223
00:09:04,570 --> 00:09:07,140
So many dinosaur fossils were found here.

224
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:09,100
They're still finding more now.

225
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:10,870
- Even now.
- Even now.

226
00:09:11,970 --> 00:09:13,600
If this is dinosaur capital,

227
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:15,370
